  <!-- ─── OVERVIEW ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <SectionTitle label="Overview" title="About Kokhe Danda Trek" />
  <div class="about-text">
          <p><strong>Kokhe Danda (3,309 m) is the highest point of Parbat district</strong>, and the Kokhe Danda Trek is a 5-day, easy trek from Pokhara that reaches it through Magar villages, rhododendron and oak forest and family-run homestays. The ridge sits next to Mohare Danda, which lies across the boundary in Myagdi district, and close to Poon Hill, but it sees far fewer trekkers, with views of Dhaulagiri, Annapurna and, on clear days, Manaslu and Langtang.</p>
      <p>The Kokhe Danda Trek is a relatively new trail, opened by us at Discover Parbat. We built it to show trekkers the Parbat we know: quiet forest trails, hilltop villages and families who still live much as their grandparents did. What sets it apart from better-known routes is not only the scenery but the chance to step into local culture and daily life rather than simply passing through it.</p>
      <p>Your first night is at Haljure, in a truly authentic homestay. This is a traditional village home, not a lodge built for tourists. Dinner is cooked by your host in a traditional Nepali kitchen, often over a wood fire, and you are welcome to sit beside the hearth while dal bhat, seasonal vegetables and fresh rotis are prepared. After the meal, a campfire is lit outside and, with almost no light pollution, the night sky fills with stars.</p>
      <p>At Lespar, you can join a momo-making session with your hosts, learning to fold Nepal's favourite dumplings by hand before eating them together. Spend free time exploring the village on foot, walking between terraced fields and stone-and-mud houses, and chatting with locals about farming, festivals and life in the hills. These small, unplanned moments are often what trekkers remember most.</p>
      <p>Because the trail is still new, it sees very few tourists. On most departures you will have the trail and the homestays practically to yourselves, with no queues at viewpoints, no crowded dining halls and no competition for the best seat at sunrise. It is a rare kind of solitude in the Annapurna foothills, only a short drive from Pokhara.</p>
      <p>Our guides are local to the region and were part of the team that built this trail. They know every path, village and family along the route, and they bring deep knowledge of the area's history, plants, wildlife and Magar traditions. Walking with them feels less like a guided tour and more like being shown around by friends from home.</p>
      <p>The reward at the top is a sweeping Himalayan panorama. From Kokhe Danda, the view stretches from Dhaulagiri and the Annapurna range across to Manaslu and, on clear days, as far as the Langtang range. Sunrise paints the snow peaks gold while the valleys below are still in shadow, and sunsets from the ridge are just as unforgettable. take in Nepal's mountains far from the crowds. You may also see the ridge written as Kokhe Dada or Khoke Danda; all of these names refer to the same place.</p>
   
          <p>Want to know how this trail came to be? Read the full story of the trek, from the first idea to the people who built it, on <a href="https://kokhedandatrek.com/About-The-Trek" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kokhe Danda Trek: A Journey of Vision, Silence &amp; Soul</a>.</p>
    </div>

